# The ledger of `json/patch`: what the JSON Patch and JSON Merge Patch
# libraries of the three languages let a program do, one block per capability,
# and what Rebel answers. None of the three standard libraries has either, so
# the yardstick is the author's of 2026-11-17: Python's `jsonpatch` 1.47 (with
# the `jsonpointer` 3.12 it stands on), Go's `github.com/evanphx/json-patch/v5`
# v5.9.11 and Rust's `json-patch` 5.0.0. The lists are this machine's,
# 2026-11-17: `dir()` of the two Python modules installed in the scratch
# folder, `go doc -all` of the Go module fetched there, and the item list
# docs.rs prints for the crate. The tests are RFC 6902's examples (Appendix A,
# as the json-patch-tests suite writes them, `spec_tests.json`), that suite's
# own `tests.json`, and RFC 7386's examples (Appendix A), every one.

capability patch/operations  the six operations of a JSON Patch held as values a program matches on
  heroes  json/patch.Operation
  go      Operation, Operation.Kind, Operation.Path, Operation.From, Operation.ValueInterface
  rust    PatchOperation, AddOperation, RemoveOperation, ReplaceOperation, MoveOperation, CopyOperation, TestOperation, Patch
  python  PatchOperation, AddOperation, RemoveOperation, ReplaceOperation, MoveOperation, CopyOperation, TestOperation, PatchOperation.path, PatchOperation.key, JsonPatch.operations
  note    a variant of six cases, so a `match` answers all six; the RFC's `test` is the case `test_value`, `test` being a word the language keeps for itself

capability patch/parse  a patch read from its JSON text or from a value already read
  heroes  json/patch.parse, json/patch.from_value
  go      DecodePatch, ErrBadJSONPatch
  rust    serde_json::from_value::<Patch>
  python  JsonPatch.from_string, JsonPatch(list), JsonPatch.json_loader, InvalidJsonPatch
  note    a member an operation does not read is not read, as RFC 6902 § 4 asks; a missing `value` is an error and a `value` of `null` is a value. A text with a key twice in one object fails as `json.parse` fails, so RFC 6902 A.13's patch with two `op` members is refused before it is read, which the RFC asks

capability patch/format  a patch written as JSON
  heroes  json/patch.to_value, json/patch.format
  go      json.Marshal of a Patch
  rust    serde_json::to_value of a Patch
  python  JsonPatch.to_string, JsonPatch.json_dumper
  note    `op`, then `from` and `path`, then `value`, the order of RFC 6902's examples

capability patch/apply  a patch applied to a value, every operation or none
  heroes  json/patch.apply
  go      Patch.Apply, Patch.ApplyWithOptions, Patch.ApplyIndent, Patch.ApplyIndentWithOptions, ErrMissing, ErrInvalid, ErrInvalidIndex, ErrUnknownType, ErrExpectedObject, ErrBadJSONDoc, ArraySizeError, NewArraySizeError
  rust    patch, PatchError, PatchErrorKind
  python  apply_patch, JsonPatch.apply, PatchOperation.apply, JsonPatchException, JsonPatchConflict, JsonPatchPathNotFound
  note    every operation or none, as RFC 6902 § 5 asks and as Python's `atomic=True` and Rust's `patch` do; Python's default and Rust's `patch_unsafe` leave a half-changed document. A path naming nothing is `not_found`, a token that is no index or a path into a value that holds nothing `invalid`; each message names the operation by its index. An index with a leading zero is refused, as the RFC's grammar writes it, where Go reads `/01` as 1 (observed); taking out the whole value is refused, as Go refuses it (observed). The json-patch-tests suite's 95 tests and 17 of RFC 6902 pass, the four it disables included

capability patch/test  a patch that fails where the value is not what it expects
  heroes  json/patch.Operation (`test_value`)
  go      Operation of kind "test", ErrTestFailed
  rust    TestOperation, PatchErrorKind::TestFailed
  python  TestOperation, JsonPatchTestFailed
  note    numbers compared by value and objects whatever their order, on a stack, so a value nested past what `==` follows is compared too; a failing test is `invalid`, the message saying what was expected and what was found

capability patch/apply-loose  what RFC 6902 refuses, applied because the caller asked
  heroes  json/patch.apply_with, json/patch.ApplyOptions, json/patch.strict
  go      ApplyOptions, NewApplyOptions, ApplyOptions.SupportNegativeIndices, ApplyOptions.AllowMissingPathOnRemove, ApplyOptions.EnsurePathExistsOnAdd, SupportNegativeIndices
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    Go's three options, each off unless asked, where Go's `SupportNegativeIndices` is on by default; a parent made where asked is a list where the token after it is an index or `-`, and an object otherwise, as Go makes them (observed: `/a/0/b` on `{}` gives `{"a":[{"b":1}]}`)

capability patch/diff  the patch that turns one value into another
  heroes  json/patch.diff
  go      -
  rust    diff
  python  make_patch, JsonPatch.from_diff, DiffBuilder
  note    objects member by member; a list keeps what it shares at its start and its end and changes its middle element by element, then grows or cuts it, so an element put in or taken out is one operation. Python's `make_patch` also finds moves, which shortens a patch and never changes what it does; no two of the three agree on the operations, only on the result, which the tests hold

capability patch/diff-tested  the patch made with a test before each change, so it fails on a value that moved since
  heroes  json/patch.diff_with_tests
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  make_patch(generate_test_ops=True), JsonPatch.from_diff(generate_test_ops=True)
  note    a `test` before each `remove` and `replace`, as Python's `generate_test_ops` writes them

capability patch/merge  a JSON Merge Patch applied to a value
  heroes  json/patch.merge
  go      MergePatch
  rust    merge
  python  -
  note    RFC 7386 § 2, its fifteen examples as tests; a value nested deep is merged on a stack

capability patch/merge-diff  the merge patch that turns one value into another
  heroes  json/patch.merge_diff
  go      CreateMergePatch
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    fails `unsupported` where the value made holds a `null` no merge patch can set: Go's `CreateMergePatch` writes `{"a":null}` for `{"a":1}` to `{"a":null}` (observed), which takes the member out instead

capability patch/merge-merge  two merge patches made one
  heroes  json/patch.compose_merges
  go      MergeMergePatches
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    fails `unsupported` where no one patch does what the two do: Go's `MergeMergePatches` answers `{"a":{"b":1}}` for `{"a":1}` then `{"a":{"b":1}}` (observed), which merges into an object `a` where the two in turn would have replaced it

capability patch/pointer-set  a value set or taken out at a JSON pointer
  heroes  json/patch.apply
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  jsonpointer.set_pointer, JsonPointer.set, PatchOperation.set_part, PatchOperation.get_part
  note    a value set at a pointer is the operation `add` or `replace`, and taken out `remove`; Python's `set_pointer` is one call of `apply`

capability patch/equal  two JSON values compared structurally
  heroes  built-in ==
  go      Equal
  rust    serde_json::Value == Value
  python  dict == dict
  note    `==` on two `json.Value`s is JSON's equality: numbers by value, objects whatever their order

dropped
  rust    patch_unsafe: an application that leaves the value half changed where an operation fails; here every application is whole or nothing
  python  apply_patch(in_place=), JsonPatch.apply(in_place=, atomic=): every value of the language is a copy, so an application never changes the caller's value and is always atomic
  python  apply_patch(pointer_cls=), make_patch(pointer_cls=), PatchOperation.pointer_cls: a class of the caller's standing in for JsonPointer, which needs an interface
  go      AccumulatedCopySizeLimit, ApplyOptions.AccumulatedCopySizeLimit, AccumulatedCopySizeError, NewAccumulatedCopySizeError: a guard against a patch whose copies double the document; a copy here shares the value it copies until one side changes, so a copy costs no memory until it is written
  python  JsonPatchWithAppend, AppendOperation: an operation outside RFC 6902 that appends to a string, which the RFC's `replace` already does
  python  JsonPointer, JsonPointerException, EndOfList, jsonpointer.resolve_pointer, jsonpointer.escape, jsonpointer.unescape, JsonPointer.resolve, JsonPointer.get, JsonPointer.path, JsonPointer.from_parts, JsonPointer.get_parts, JsonPointer.contains, JsonPointer.join, JsonPointer.to_last, JsonPointer.walk: RFC 6901's pointers, which are `json`'s (`json.value_at`, `json.pointer_of`, `json.pointer_tokens`)
  python  DiffBuilder.execute, DiffBuilder.insert, DiffBuilder.remove, DiffBuilder.store_index, DiffBuilder.take_index, jsonpatch.multidict, jsonpatch.path_join, jsonpatch.path_split: the machinery behind make_patch, no part of a program's use
